WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
Why Bond Prices Fall
When Interest Rates Rise

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:08.540
In 2022, Dick's Sporting Goods
borrowed money by selling bonds,

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.620
and it has made every
payment since on time.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:15.980
But by January 2026, each $1,000 of

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:19.680
those bonds was worth only $920.91.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:21.360
Let's see why.

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:24.540
We'll start with a simpler bond.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:29.120
Say you lend a company
$1,000 for five years at 5%.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:34.860
Every year, it pays you 5%
of $1,000, which is $50.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.460
That payment is called the coupon.

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:41.480
At the end of the five years,
you get your $1,000 back.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:43.480
That's the bond's face value.

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.560
Now, suppose interest
rates rise, and companies

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.080
have to pay 6% to borrow.

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:54.980
That 6% is the market rate,
the rate lenders want today.

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:58.300
A new $1,000 bond pays $60 a year

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.620
and repays the same $1,000 in the end.

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:05.013
Let's say you need your money now, so

00:01:05.063 --> 00:01:07.013
you're looking for
someone to buy your bond.

00:01:07.783 --> 00:01:10.193
Any buyer will ask the same question.

00:01:10.963 --> 00:01:14.503
Why pay $1,000 for $50
a year when $1,000 buys

00:01:14.553 --> 00:01:18.093
a brand new bond that pays $60 a year?

00:01:18.703 --> 00:01:23.653
With your bond, she'll get $10
less every year for five years.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.770
So, you'll have to take the
shortfall off the price.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.450
It comes to a little less than $50 because

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:33.270
money that arrives later
is worth less today.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:39.730
At 6%, the $10 she misses next
year is worth $9.43 today.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:47.190
The one after that is worth $8.90,
then $8.40, $7.92, and $7.47.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:50.390
Together, that's $42.12.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:02:00.050
So, your bond sells for $1,000
minus $42.12, which is $957.88.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:03.820
At that price, your old bond earns its

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.630
buyer 6%, the same as the new one.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:08.660
It works the other way, too.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:13.060
Suppose the market rate
had fallen to 4% instead.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:17.050
A new bond would pay
only $40 a year, so your

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.040
$50 is now the better deal by $10 a year.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:25.930
Buyers will pay extra
for that, and your bond

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.740
sells for more than its
face value at $1,044.52.

00:02:31.297 --> 00:02:34.327
Rising rates are what happened
to Dick's Sporting Goods lenders.

00:02:35.277 --> 00:02:40.627
Their bonds pay 3.15% a
year, and that never changes.

00:02:40.677 --> 00:02:44.457
Since 2022, interest rates have risen, and

00:02:44.507 --> 00:02:48.287
lenders now want more than 3.15%.

00:02:48.337 --> 00:02:52.207
So, the price fell to $920.91.

00:02:53.397 --> 00:02:55.647
Dick's still pays exactly
what it promised, and

00:02:55.697 --> 00:02:57.947
the lenders holding the
bonds took the loss.
